    <section class="heros-a-propos">
      <div class="conteneur">
        <h1>What is AZERTY Global?</h1>
        <p class="heros-a-propos__chapo">An improved French keyboard layout, free and open source, that fixes the historical flaws of the traditional Windows AZERTY.</p>

        <div class="heros-a-propos__intro">
          <p class="texte-ample">
            Have you ever tried to type <strong>É</strong> or <strong>Ç</strong> on your keyboard? On the traditional Windows AZERTY, it is simply impossible without obscure Alt codes or copy and paste. The same goes for the <strong>œ</strong> in “<span lang="fr">cœur</span>” or the <strong>French quotation marks « »</strong>. And the <strong>period</strong>, one of the most frequent signs in French, requires pressing <kbd>Shift</kbd>.
          </p>
          <p class="texte-ample">
            <strong>AZERTY Global makes these characters accessible</strong>, free of charge, on your current physical keyboard.
          </p>
          <p class="heros-a-propos__accroche">
            <strong>The letters A to Z stay in place, and so do the digits.</strong>
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="heros-a-propos__actions">
          <a href="/en/download" class="bouton bouton--primaire">Download</a>
          <a href="/testeur" class="bouton bouton--secondaire" hreflang="fr" data-mesure="vers_testeur" data-mesure-emplacement="a-propos-hero">Try it online<span class="visuellement-cache"> (in French)</span></a>
        </div>

        <div class="heros-a-propos__clavier">
          {{ dessin(clavierEn, "a-propos-heros", "synthese", "AZERTY Global keyboard: the letters and digits stay in place; É È À Ç with Caps Lock; the period without Shift; @ and # on the top-left key; { } [ ] \\ and | on the home row with AltGr. The cards that follow detail these changes.") }}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    {# The five changes, in the order and under the titles of the guide #}
    <section class="section">
      <div class="conteneur">
        <h2>What changes in practice</h2>

        <div class="a-propos-cartes">
          <article class="carte">
            <h3>Smart Caps Lock</h3>
            <p>
              With Caps&nbsp;Lock on, é è à ç give <span class="insecable"><kbd>É</kbd> <kbd>È</kbd> <kbd>À</kbd> <kbd>Ç</kbd></span>; digits and punctuation do not change. No more copy and paste or Alt codes. <a href="/e-aigu-majuscule" hreflang="fr">Typing accented capitals<span class="visuellement-cache"> (in French)</span></a>
            </p>
          </article>

          <article class="carte">
            <h3>The period without Shift</h3>
            <p>
              The period <kbd>.</kbd> is a direct key, as on keyboards everywhere else in the world. The semicolon, far less frequent, takes Shift on the same key.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article class="carte">
            <h3>At sign and hash at the top left</h3>
            <p>
              The <a href="/arobase" hreflang="fr">at sign<span class="visuellement-cache"> (in French)</span></a> <kbd>@</kbd> and the hash <kbd>#</kbd> are on the former ² key, left of the 1, as on the macOS AZERTY. The old AltGr + 0 still works.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article class="carte">
            <h3>Programming symbols on the home row</h3>
            <p>
              The <a href="/accolades" hreflang="fr">braces<span class="visuellement-cache"> (in French)</span></a> <span class="insecable"><kbd>{</kbd> <kbd>}</kbd></span>, the <a href="/crochets" hreflang="fr">brackets<span class="visuellement-cache"> (in French)</span></a> <span class="insecable"><kbd>[</kbd> <kbd>]</kbd></span>, the backslash <kbd>\</kbd> and the vertical bar <kbd>|</kbd> fall under your fingers with AltGr. <a href="/dev" hreflang="fr">See the symbols for developers<span class="visuellement-cache"> (in French)</span></a>
            </p>
          </article>

          <article class="carte">
            <h3>International accents on the ù key</h3>
            <p>
              Three dead-key accents take the place of the ù: acute for <span class="insecable"><kbd>á</kbd> <kbd>í</kbd> <kbd>ó</kbd> <kbd>ú</kbd></span>, grave for <span class="insecable"><kbd>ì</kbd> <kbd>ò</kbd></span>, tilde for <span class="insecable"><kbd>ã</kbd> <kbd>ñ</kbd> <kbd>õ</kbd></span>. Spanish, Italian and Portuguese can be typed directly. The <kbd>ù</kbd> moves to AltGr + U, and the percent sign moves to Shift + closing parenthesis.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article class="carte">
            <h3>And hundreds of extra characters</h3>
            <p>
              This is not a change: it is what the traditional AZERTY was missing. The <a href="/guillemets" hreflang="fr">French quotation marks<span class="visuellement-cache"> (in French)</span></a> <span class="insecable"><kbd>«</kbd> <kbd>»</kbd></span>, the <a href="/tiret-cadratin" hreflang="fr">em dash<span class="visuellement-cache"> (in French)</span></a> <kbd>—</kbd>, the <a href="/e-dans-l-o" hreflang="fr">œ ligature<span class="visuellement-cache"> (in French)</span></a> <kbd>œ</kbd>, the <a href="/e-dans-l-a" hreflang="fr">æ ligature<span class="visuellement-cache"> (in French)</span></a> <kbd>æ</kbd>, nonbreaking spaces, and the German <kbd>ß</kbd> or the Danish <kbd>ø</kbd>.
            </p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

