    {# Behind the project #}
    <section class="section">
      <div class="conteneur a-propos-colonne">
        <h2>Behind the project</h2>
        <div class="carte recit">
          <figure class="recit__photo">
            <picture>
              <source srcset="/images/antoine-clavier-450.webp" type="image/webp">
              <img src="/images/antoine-clavier-450.jpg"
                alt="Antoine Olivier holding a keyboard with the AZERTY Global logo"
                width="450" height="451" loading="lazy">
            </picture>
            <figcaption>Antoine Olivier, with the association’s demo keyboard.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <p class="texte-ample">
            AZERTY Global was born in 2017 from one student’s irritation: Antoine Olivier, then in
            French preparatory classes, was tired of hunting for an É or French quotation marks on his
            keyboard. He co-founded the project with Robert Hodge, who drew the first prototype, then
            developed it from 2018. Several versions later, the layout is published free of charge
            under a free license by an association, and he is still the one who moves it forward.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    {# Who runs the project #}
    <section class="section">
      <div class="conteneur a-propos-colonne">
        <h2>Who runs the project</h2>
        <div class="carte">
          <p class="texte-ample">
            Since 2026, AZERTY Global has been run by the <strong>Association pour la Modernisation du Clavier
            Français</strong> (AMCF), a French nonprofit association under the <em>loi 1901</em>, founded in
            Clermont-Ferrand, declared at the Riom sub-prefecture and published in the <em>Journal officiel des
            associations</em>. It publishes the layout, the tools and the educational resources, free of charge and
            under a free license. It is entirely volunteer-run.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a href="/en/association" class="bouton bouton--secondaire">More about the association</a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    {# Foundations #}
    <section class="section">
      <div class="conteneur a-propos-colonne">
        <h2>The foundations of AZERTY Global</h2>
        <div class="carte">
          <p class="texte-ample">
            AZERTY Global’s design draws on several sources:
          </p>
          <ul class="liste-faits">
            <li><strong>AFNOR public survey from June 2017</strong>: feedback from the public on
              AZERTY keyboard problems shaped the development priorities.</li>
            <li><strong>Study of foreign layouts</strong>: inspiration from international best practices,
              such as dead keys on the number row (Hungarian layout) or Smart Caps Lock
              (Spanish QWERTY).</li>
            <li><strong>Intuition and daily practice</strong>: daily use and continuous improvement of
              the layout by Antoine Olivier, who has developed it since 2018.</li>
            <li><strong>Community feedback</strong>: feedback from early users has shaped the
              successive versions.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    {# Quick questions (.notice component), right before the exit cards #}
    <section class="section">
      <div class="conteneur a-propos-colonne">
        <h2>Quick questions</h2>

        <details class="notice">
          <summary>Is it free?</summary>
          <div class="notice__contenu prose">
            <p><strong>Yes. It is free and open source.</strong> The code is available on GitHub under the <a href="/licence" hreflang="fr">EUPL 1.2 license<span class="visuellement-cache"> (in French)</span></a>.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="notice">
          <summary>Do I need to buy a new keyboard?</summary>
          <div class="notice__contenu prose">
            <p><strong>No.</strong> AZERTY Global is a purely software solution. It works with your current keyboard.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="notice">
          <summary>Is it compatible with games (ZQSD)?</summary>
          <div class="notice__contenu prose">
            <p><strong>Yes.</strong> The physical Z, Q, S, D keys do not move. Some games, however, depend on the console key, a shortcut or an anti-cheat system: the <a href="/en/compatibility">compatibility page</a> lists the known cases.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="notice">
          <summary>Is it available on Mac and Linux?</summary>
          <div class="notice__contenu prose">
            <p><strong>Yes!</strong> AZERTY Global is available on <strong>Windows</strong> (Windows 10 version 1809 or later, and Windows 11), <strong>macOS</strong> (10.12 or later) and <strong>Linux</strong> (Ubuntu, Debian, Fedora, Arch, etc.).</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="notice">
          <summary>How long does it take to adjust?</summary>
          <div class="notice__contenu prose">
            <p>A few days of use for the reflexes to settle in. The letters A to Z and the digits stay in place; what takes some adjustment is the five changes. The <a href="/en/guide">guide</a> presents them and offers a cheat sheet.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="notice">
          <summary>Who created AZERTY Global?</summary>
          <div class="notice__contenu prose">
            <p><strong>Robert Hodge and Antoine Olivier</strong>, who co-founded it in 2017. Antoine Olivier, then a student in a French preparatory class and frustrated by the impossibility of typing correctly in French on his keyboard, has developed it since 2018. The project is now run by the AMCF (Association pour la Modernisation du Clavier Français) and remains volunteer-run.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>

